/* ATTR_PROJECTILE_ARC */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain SNOW

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_60 - does a DIFFERENT word with the same value open a comment, or is the
shore fish a special case?
Feeds: parser-design Sec.2.1 and Sec.13 item 7, the token-alias import

THIS HEADER IS AT THE BOTTOM. The test is the first line.

WHAT IS ALREADY MEASURED, IN ORDER
  RMSTEST_56a  the shore-fish constant alone in a leading comment -> blank map.
               The word opens a nested comment; comments nest, so the closing
               marker shuts only the inner one and the file is never read.
  RMSTEST_56b  the same file with an empty comment -> snow. The control.
  RMSTEST_57   the bare literal 69 in a leading comment -> SNOW. So it is NOT
               "any token whose value is 69". Numeric literals are lexed as
               numbers and never reach the symbol table.

So the path is words. What is not known is whether it is words IN GENERAL that
carry the value, or that one name.

THE TWO NAMES, AND WHY THIS PAIR
Both are #const lines in the engine's own random_map.def, and both are 69. The
file is readable straight from the install, no archive extraction needed:

  <install>/resources/_common/drs/gamedata_x2/random_map.def

  line  263:  #const  SHORE_FISH          69     object constants section
  line 1117:  #const  ATTR_PROJECTILE_ARC 69     attribute constants section

Those are the ONLY two. The file was searched for every constant with that
value, so this run is not a sample of a class - it is the whole of the remaining
candidate set. If it blanks, the complete list of engine-defined words that can
destroy a file from inside a comment is those two, plus whatever a script
#const's for itself, which the parser already tracks.

The same file also shows where /* does NOT get its number: nothing defines it
there, and the markers appear only as ordinary comments. So the comment-marker
ids live in the engine's internal token table, and the id for */ is still
unknown - that half needs the equivalencies sheet and this run does not.

Ids in that file are per-namespace and collide freely - 45 is DOCK and CUSTOM
and CIVILIZATION_GEORGIANS and ATTR_BLAST_DEFENSE. That collision is normally a
nuisance. Here it is the instrument: two words the engine defines as the same
number, from two different namespaces, so the run separates the VALUE from the
namespace.

  BLANK or grass map -> a word valued 69 opens a comment, whatever kind of
                        constant it is. The rule is about the number, the fix is
                        a lookup keyed on it, and the useful output of the
                        token-alias import is the set of names equal to the /*
                        and */ ids rather than the whole sheet.
  SNOW map           -> the value alone is not sufficient for words either. Then
                        either the object namespace is privileged in this
                        position, or the shore fish is special for a reason not
                        yet identified, and Sec.13 item 7 needs rethinking
                        rather than finishing.

RMSTEST_56b is already the control for this file - same script, empty comment,
snow - so no new control is needed. If this comes back blank, 56b is what proves
the blankness came from the word.

WHY IT IS WORTH ONE GENERATION
It is the difference between a two-entry lookup and a hard-coded exception. A
parser diagnostic for this currently cannot exist at all: the parser treats
comments as a token-stream construct and resolves no constants inside them, so it
reports a comment where the engine reports the end of the file. That is a silent
wrong answer about how much of the file exists, which is the worst shape a parser
bug can take, and it has already cost one run in this directory.

A NOTE ON HOW THIS FINDING HAS GONE SO FAR, BECAUSE IT IS THE THIRD ATTEMPT
The first write-up said "an object constant in a comment can delete the script" -
a class of names, generalised from one observation. The second said "it is one
number, /* is 69 and so is the fish" - right about the shape, wrong about the
scope, and written into four documents before RMSTEST_57 refuted the literal half
of it. Each step was reasonable and two of three were wrong. This file exists so
the third does not go the same way.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. ONE RUN. Binary.

READ IT WITH
  Look at the map. Snow or not snow is the whole reading.
*/
